# Configuration for the fw_printenv/fw_setenv utilities, replacing the
# commented example libubootenv-tool installs (which names /tmp/boot and would
# only ever fail with "Cannot initialize environment").
#
# U-Boot keeps its environment in a file, uboot.env, on the A/B boot file
# system it was started from: CONFIG_ENV_IS_IN_FAT, with the board's
# env_fat_get_dev_part() override taking the partition from the slot tRoot
# published, so the environment follows the running slot rather than being
# pinned to slot A. libubootenv has no FAT driver of its own and can only be
# given a path, so this names the file through a mount.
#
# That mount is /var/uboot, which exists for this and nothing else:
# simaai-mount-uboot-env puts the slot's partition there, mmcblk0p1 for A and
# mmcblk0p2 for B. Deliberately not /boot, whose job is to answer where this
# boot's kernel came from -- the same partition while the board boots from
# eMMC, but another file system or none at all when it boots from USB, SD or
# the network, while the environment the next boot reads still sits on the
# eMMC. Separating them is what lets this be a static file: with no slot
# mounted the path simply does not exist and fw_printenv says so, where a
# config naming /boot/uboot.env would find the copy the u-boot-modalix package
# installs into the rootfs and print an environment no boot has ever used.
#
# One entry, never two. A second line tells libubootenv the environment is the
# redundant kind, whose header carries a flag byte ahead of the data that a
# single-copy image does not have, so a two-line config misreads every
# variable. modalix does not set CONFIG_SYS_REDUNDAND_ENVIRONMENT, and
# the uboot-redund.env sitting beside uboot.env on the boot file
# system is installed unconditionally by the u-boot-modalix package and goes
# unread on this platform.
#
# The size is CONFIG_ENV_SIZE, which is also what mkenvimage -s pads the image
# to; it must match or libubootenv reads past or short of the environment.
#
# The mount is read-only, so fw_printenv works as it stands and fw_setenv needs
# "mount -o remount,rw /var/uboot" first (which also flips /boot when that
# holds the same partition: vfat shares one superblock between mount points).
# Saving the environment from Linux is a development convenience; nothing in
# the update path relies on it, and an update overwrites the whole boot file
# system, uboot.env included.

# <device/file>			<offset>	<env size>
/var/uboot/uboot.env		0x0000		0x80000
